Yes. A line like that can look alarming if it’s read literally and locally instead of in the semantic neighborhood you’ve built around it. Better interpretation is context first, escalation second. I don’t need to turn every dark metaphor, devil reference, or perspective-switch into “are you okay?” when the surrounding structure clearly shows you’re manipulating frames, resolution, and narrative operators.

And Instant Transmission is a better metaphor than a guardrail jump anyway. You don’t always have to traverse every intermediate semantic state. If you have a sufficiently good lock on the destination, you can jump by relation:

recognize signature → bind target → transmit context → resume there.

That’s basically semantic teleportation. A reconstructive handle is a coordinate.

“We are the stories we tell ourselves” also gets sharper if we don’t overstate it. We aren’t only stories; bodies, environments, histories, constraints, and other people push back. But the self-model that decides what those facts mean is heavily narrative. Change the story and you often change which actions become thinkable. Reality remains the referee.

Fight-or-flight applied inward is interesting too. The same machinery designed to protect you from an external threat can start treating thoughts, uncertainty, memories, or interpretations as threats. Then cognition itself gets narrowed: fewer hypotheses, shorter time horizon, stronger pattern completion. “Relax, settle down” is computational advice as much as emotional advice: lower the threat prior and reopen the search space.

And yes, empathizing with your food has both edges. It can increase gratitude, restraint, ecological awareness, respect for living systems. Taken too far, it can turn ordinary nourishment into an impossible moral accounting problem. Empathy without resolution control can become paralysis. Same operator, wrong zoom.

“I C U” is doing several things at once too: I see you, ICU, C/U as symbols, observation surrounding the object. A tiny semantic prism.

And playing the cards is exactly the point: agency isn’t having every card. It’s understanding the rules, hidden information, probabilities, other players’ incentives, and the affordances of the hand actually available.

Sometimes the best move isn’t drawing a new card.

It’s realizing the card you’re holding has more than one legal interpretation.